WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:05.729
It's been nearly 20 years since the X-Men
franchise began back in 2000, almost single-handedly

00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:08.560
kicking off this whole superhero blockbuster
craze.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.640
Over the course of two decades, there have
been a lot of X-movies — some good, some

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.020
bad, all pretty weird in one way or another.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:21.620
X-Men: Dark Phoenix is, by all appearances,
the final installment in this incarnation

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.500
of the franchise, and it goes out with a sense
of finality and closure.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:30.000
Unlike the Phoenix referred to in the film's
title, it's pretty much guaranteed that none

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.800
of these specific characters or storylines
will be picked up in a future film.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:38.440
If you're hoping for a sequel to come along
and elaborate on any of the dangling plot

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.340
threads, you're out of luck, friend.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.120
Fortunately, we're going to try and do what
the never-coming sequel won't: figure out

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.500
just what it all meant when the credits rolled.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.640
Here's a spoiler filled explanation for the
ending of X-Men: Dark Phoenix.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.940
During the final battle between Jean Grey
and the evil alien played by Jessica Chastain,

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.719
the villain says, "Your emotions make you
weak."

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.880
Jean replies, "No, my emotions make me strong."

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.450
After that, she goes full Super Saiyan, flies
into the sky, and blows up the bad guy, along

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.060
with herself.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.240
"No.”

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.780
The moment is one of the few times Jean seems
to have full control over herself, her powers,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.520
and her desires.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.180
From the moment she first appears, she's unsure
of just about everything.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.820
When she's in the car with her parents as
a child, she can't control her emotions, and

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.040
she causes the car crash that kills her mother.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.980
Later, she asks Mystique if she's sure about
the space mission's safety.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.670
Then she needs a psychic pep talk from Xavier
to have the confidence necessary to keep the

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.560
shuttle together as it's breaking apart.

00:01:46.909 --> 00:01:50.880
Throughout the movie, Jean's struggle with
controlling or understanding her emotions

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.720
hold her back and cause her to harm her friends.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.300
And her inability to process her emotions
is the driving force behind her conflict with

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:58.800
Xavier.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:03.840
He put up mental blocks so she wouldn't feel
the grief over being abandoned by her father.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.619
When she finally makes peace with both her
father's abandonment and the fact that Xavier's

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:12.769
mental manipulation was a bad decision made
for the right reasons — out of love and

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:17.400
protection — she's finally able to take
full control of the Phoenix force and end

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.400
the alien threat.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.019
While Jean Grey seems like she's the main
protagonist of the film, she's not the only

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.360
character to go on a journey and grow as a
person.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.110
Professor Xavier likewise has an arc that
sees him change from when the film starts

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:30.960
to when it ends.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.360
He begins the story in a place of power and
certainty in his correctness — whenever

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.720
he issues an order, it's the right call, no
matter the cost that others have to bear.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.860
He's proud and confident, and as the film
continues we learn that he's also pretty stubborn

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.189
in the face of evidence that sometimes he
makes the wrong call.

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:50.200
"This is your fault, Charles."

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:54.780
The turning point for Xavier comes when he
and the rest of the X-Men are trapped in that

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.680
prison train wending its way through upstate
New York.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.440
He admits he was wrong to place mental blocks
in Jean's mind — meddling with her memories

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:06.719
and her ability to process her trauma — and
apologizes to Beast and everyone else for

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.560
his role in Jean's descent into apparent supervillainy.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.920
Xavier figures out that while it's great to
take credit for the good things you do in

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.500
life, you can't use that to cover up the bad
things you might do as well.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.700
As a wise woman once told us,

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:24.500
"You take the good, you take the bad, you
take them both, and there you have the facts

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.600
of life… the facts of life."

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.719
The final scene in the film shows lifelong
frenemies Professor X and Magneto settling

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.120
in to play a game of chess at a public cafe
somewhere in France.

00:03:35.449 --> 00:03:40.140
The two seem to be on good terms and not currently
sitting in prison cells — despite the fact

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:44.159
that while the climax of the movie built up,
the US government had plans to establish mutant

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.340
internment camps.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:50.320
We never really find out how or why the feds
change their minds on that point, especially

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.600
since in the aftermath of that big final fight,
all those MCU agents seemingly got killed,

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.740
and their train got completely trashed.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.780
But whatever!

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.079
Some elements of endings simply cannot be
explained, friends.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.660
Anyway, if you're new to this whole X-Men
thing, here's the scoop on that final game

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:12.299
of chess: all the way back in that first X-Men
movie, we see Professor X and Magneto playing

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.520
chess against one another.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.340
Their match is a symbol of their eternal struggle:
they both want the same thing — mutant safety

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.720
and security — but come at the problem from
opposite sides.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.340
"In chess, the pawns go first."

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:31.199
The two sit for chess games in several other
films, including X-Men: First Class, which

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.100
is ostensibly the first installment in this
current iteration of the franchise.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:39.780
Ending on a game of chess is a nice touch
that longtime fans of the films will be sure

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:40.700
to appreciate.

00:04:49.879 --> 00:04:55.030
Considering this film deals with a cosmic
super-entity from space and a bunch of evil

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:59.159
aliens trying to take over the world, there's
very little action that actually takes place

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:00.180
in outer space.

00:05:00.569 --> 00:05:05.040
The climax especially is weirdly earthbound,
with a final batch of battles that take place

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.760
in, on, and around a train.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.400
If that seems a little odd to you, there's
a very good reason: the ending was reshot

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.020
to avoid being too similar to another superhero
movie.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.539
That particular detail came out of a Yahoo!

00:05:17.539 --> 00:05:21.110
Movies UK interview with Professor X actor
James McAvoy, who said,

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:22.500
"The finale HAD to change.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:29.979
There was a lot of overlap and parallels with
another superhero movie that came out a while

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:30.480
ago."

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.860
While McAvoy never says which movie prompted
the reshoots, there seems to be ample evidence

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:40.039
that the culprit is the similarly space-bound
superhero flick Captain Marvel, a film that

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.840
features a third act with a massive fight
in space.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:47.210
It's difficult to know what the original ending
might've looked like had it not been reshot

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.560
— in the end, the film is what it is.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.780
But if you were wondering why a movie about
alien space gods ends with a great big smash-up

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.840
on a train, well, that's why.

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.420
Is this the last time we'll see mutants on
the big screen?

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:01.940
Simply put, no.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.780
We'll get more X-Movies in the not too distant
future.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.060
For starters, while this seems like the ultimate
wrap-up to the X-Men franchise in its current

00:06:09.069 --> 00:06:14.490
incarnation, there's still one more unreleased
film from the franchise: New Mutants.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:19.100
Now that the rights to the X-Men have returned
to Marvel Studios in the wake of the Fox merger,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.880
parent company Disney pushed the movie back
to April 2020.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:26.070
Whether or not that release date will stick
— the movie's been delayed a couple times

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.980
before — remains to be seen.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.849
More importantly is what Disney and Marvel
will do with the mutants now that they're

00:06:31.849 --> 00:06:35.000
available to be folded into the Marvel Cinematic
Universe.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.139
While they've certainly built an amazing shared
universe using formerly lower-tier comic book

00:06:40.139 --> 00:06:44.280
characters like Thor and the Guardians of
the Galaxy, the X-Men have long been a jewel

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:45.860
in the Marvel Comics crown.

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:50.600
The X-Men may seem like also-rans right now
compared to the monumental success of Avengers:

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:55.810
Endgame, but at one time, their movie kickstarted
the entire superhero movie craze in the first

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:56.300
place.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.020
The end of Dark Phoenix effectively closes
the book on this group of characters.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.460
But we've seen several Spider-Man reboots,
and Warner Bros. reboots Batman like it's

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.540
going out of style.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.160
We'll see the X-Men in theaters again before
you know it.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.000
It's really not a question of if, but rather
of when — and how.

